---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

// Status → badge color
const statusStyle = {
  "Ongoing": "badge-info",
  "In preparation": "badge-warning",
  "Submitted": "badge-secondary",
  "Accepted": "badge-success",
  "Published": "badge-success",
};

const projects = [
  {
    id: "hd163296",
    short: "Disk dust & shadows",
    emoji: "🪐",
    title: "Dust, heat and shadows in the HD 163296 disk",
    status: "Ongoing",
    place: "Max Planck Institute for Astronomy",
    when: "Summer 2026",
    team: "Advised by Kiyoaki Doi, Haochang Jiang and Myriam Benisty",
    overview: [
      "HD 163296 has an outer ring that ALMA sees clearly at millimeter wavelengths, but SPHERE can't find it in scattered light. It is also colder than a passively heated disk should be. Our idea is that a puffed-up inner disk is casting a shadow over it.",
      "To test that, I built a model where the gas density, dust settling and RADMC-3D temperature are iterated until they agree. The first runs wouldn't converge because noise kept building up in the optically thick inner disk, so I extrapolated the temperature inward from a power-law fit, which fixed it. The converged model now reproduces the near-infrared rings, and a dip in turbulence is enough to make the disk shadow itself.",
    ],
    numbers: [
      { value: "10⁻⁵ – 10⁻¹", label: "turbulence strengths (α) tested" },
      { value: "2.18 μm", label: "synthetic polarized images compared with SPHERE" },
    ],
    note: "Preliminary. Dust settling is messy, and we're still working it out.",
    figures: [
      { src: "/research/hd163296-observed-dichotomy.webp", alt: "ALMA millimeter image of HD 163296 showing several bright rings next to a SPHERE near-infrared image where the outer ring is absent", caption: "ALMA at 1.25 mm (left, Isella et al. 2018) sees the outer rings. SPHERE at 1.6 μm (right, Muro-Arena et al. 2018) doesn't." },
      { src: "/research/hd163296-self-shadowing.webp", alt: "Diagram of a hot puffed-up inner disk shadowing a cold settled outer disk", caption: "The hypothesis: a puffed-up inner disk shadows a cold, settled outer disk." },
      { src: "/research/hd163296-self-consistent-loop.webp", alt: "Flow chart showing temperature, dust density and RADMC-3D feeding into each other until convergence", caption: "Temperature and dust density are solved in a loop until they agree." },
      { src: "/research/hd163296-model-vs-observation.webp", alt: "Synthetic model polarized-light image next to the SPHERE observation of HD 163296", caption: "Model polarized-light image (left) next to SPHERE (right)." },
    ],
    links: [],
  },
  {
    id: "moon",
    short: "Moon formation",
    emoji: "🌕",
    title: "Building the Moon around a piece of Theia's core",
    status: "In preparation",
    place: "Michigan State University",
    when: "First author",
    team: "With Gabriel I. Nathan (NASA JPL) and Seth A. Jacobson (MSU)",
    overview: [
      "Giant-impact simulations get the Earth–Moon orbit right, but the Moon's chemistry is harder. Its mantle has about 9 wt% FeO and its core is tiny. On top of that, the Moon and Earth have almost the same isotopes.",
      "I grew Moons one moonlet at a time, letting metal sink to the core after each impact, and tried three recipes for what the moonlets were made of. Pure Earth-mantle disks never worked, and neither did well-mixed Earth–Theia disks. Growing the Moon around a leftover piece of Theia's core did. It fits the FeO, the SiO₂ and the core size together, while keeping the Moon mostly Earth material.",
    ],
    numbers: [
      { value: "1.7%", label: "of the Moon's mass is core, the target to hit" },
      { value: "> 80%", label: "proto-Earth material in the best-fit Moons" },
      { value: "1000s", label: "of simulations, with water from 0.02 to 20 wt%" },
    ],
    note: "A Theia-core remnant is a proposal. It points to a grazing, high-velocity impact, which still needs to be tested with impact simulations.",
    figures: [
      { src: "/research/moon-experiment-schematic.webp", alt: "Schematic of three experiments building a Moon from moonlets of different compositions", caption: "Three recipes: an Earth-only disk, mixed Earth–Theia disks, and a Moon grown around a Theia-core seed." },
      { src: "/research/moon-theia-core-seed-fits.webp", alt: "Goodness of fit versus first-body metal fraction for five Theia compositions", caption: "Fit quality (χ², lower is better) for a Moon grown around a Theia-core remnant, for five Theia compositions." },
      { src: "/research/moon-bse-disk-fails.webp", alt: "Plots of core mass, FeO and SiO2 versus initial water for Earth-only disks and disks with added metal", caption: "Earth-only disks (top) miss the lunar FeO and core. Adding a little metal (bottom) helps dry models. Grey bands are observations." },
    ],
    links: [],
  },
  {
    id: "earth",
    short: "Earth & Theia",
    emoji: "🌍",
    title: "Earth's accretion history across various formation scenarios",
    status: "Accepted",
    place: "NASA JPL and Michigan State University",
    when: "Second author · Icarus",
    team: "Led by Gabriel I. Nathan (NASA JPL), with Seth A. Jacobson (MSU)",
    overview: [
      "Earth's mantle keeps a record of how much oxygen was around while the planet grew. We took simulated Earths from six different planet-formation scenarios and solved backwards for the disk chemistry each one needed to end up like the real Earth.",
      "All six scenarios can work. What they have in common is that Earth swept up more and more oxidized material as it grew, with a sharp switch from reduced to oxidized material near 1 au. The fits also point to a fairly small Theia.",
    ],
    numbers: [
      { value: "164", label: "Earth analogs from six formation scenarios" },
      { value: "0.1 – 0.2 au", label: "width of the redox transition near 1 au" },
      { value: "< 0.3", label: "Theia's mass relative to the proto-Earth" },
    ],
    note: "The model is flexible, so fitting Earth doesn't prove any one scenario. Whether such a sharp redox jump is physical is still open.",
    figures: [
      { src: "/research/earth-theia-size-schematic.webp", alt: "Two schematics of a small and a large Theia hitting the Proto-Earth with ranges of mantle FeO", caption: "A small Theia (left) allows a wide range of compositions. An Earth-sized Theia (right) has to look like Earth." },
      { src: "/research/earth-multistage-differentiation.webp", alt: "Diagram of a projectile embryo hitting a target embryo, creating a melt pond where metal and silicate equilibrate", caption: "Each impact makes a melt pond where metal and silicate equilibrate before the metal sinks." },
      { src: "/research/earth-best-fit-gradients.webp", alt: "Best-fit iron and silicon oxidation gradients versus distance for Grand Tack, Classical and Annulus scenarios", caption: "Best-fit disk redox gradients: reduced inside about 1 au, oxidized beyond." },
      { src: "/research/earth-fe-metal-during-accretion.webp", alt: "Fraction of iron in metal in accreted material versus Earth mass accreted for six scenarios", caption: "The metal fraction of what Earth accretes drops as it grows." },
    ],
    links: [],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Research" sideBarActiveItemID="projects">
  <div class="w-full">
    <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-6xl font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-4 bg-gradient-to-r from-primary to-secondary bg-clip-text text-transparent">
      Research
    </h1>
    <div class="h-1.5 w-24 rounded-full bg-primary mb-6"></div>
    <p class="text-xl md:text-2xl leading-relaxed mb-6">
      I study how planets and moons get their chemistry and structure, from the dust in young disks
      to the giant impacts that built Earth and the Moon. Most of what I do is numerical simulation.
    </p>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mb-14">
      {projects.map((p) => (
        <a href={`#${p.id}`} class="btn btn-outline btn-primary btn-md text-base normal-case">
          {p.emoji} {p.short}
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-20">
      {projects.map((p) => {
        const [lead, ...more] = p.figures;
        return (
          <article id={p.id} class="w-full scroll-mt-6 border-t-4 border-primary/40 pt-10">
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
              <span class={`badge badge-lg ${statusStyle[p.status]}`}>{p.status}</span>
              <span class="badge badge-lg badge-ghost">{p.place}</span>
              <span class="badge badge-lg badge-ghost">{p.when}</span>
            </div>

            <h2 class="mt-4 text-4xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold leading-tight tracking-tight">{p.title}</h2>
            <div class="h-1 w-16 rounded-full bg-primary mt-3"></div>
            <div class="text-lg text-base-content/60 mt-3">{p.team}</div>

            <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-xl md:text-2xl leading-relaxed">
              {p.overview.map((para) => <p>{para}</p>)}
            </div>

            <dl class="mt-8 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
              {p.numbers.map((n) => (
                <div class="border-l-4 border-primary pl-4">
                  <dt class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-primary">{n.value}</dt>
                  <dd class="text-lg text-base-content/80 leading-snug mt-1">{n.label}</dd>
                </div>
              ))}
            </dl>

            {lead && (
              <figure class="mt-10">
                <a href={lead.src} target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size">
                  <img
                    src={lead.src}
                    alt={lead.alt}
                    loading="lazy"
                    class="w-full h-auto max-h-[85vh] object-contain mx-auto rounded bg-white"
                  />
                </a>
                <figcaption class="text-lg md:text-xl text-base-content/80 mt-3 leading-snug">{lead.caption}</figcaption>
              </figure>
            )}

            {more.length > 0 && (
              <details class="mt-6">
                <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-bold text-primary">
                  More figures ({more.length})
                </summary>
                <div class="space-y-8 mt-6">
                  {more.map((f) => (
                    <figure>
                      <a href={f.src} target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size">
                        <img
                          src={f.src}
                          alt={f.alt}
                          loading="lazy"
                          class="w-full h-auto max-h-[85vh] object-contain mx-auto rounded bg-white"
                        />
                      </a>
                      <figcaption class="text-lg md:text-xl text-base-content/80 mt-3 leading-snug">{f.caption}</figcaption>
                    </figure>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </details>
            )}

            <div role="note" class="alert mt-8 text-lg md:text-xl">
              <span><strong>Caveat:</strong> {p.note}</span>
            </div>

            {p.links.length > 0 && (
              <div class="card-actions mt-4">
                {p.links.map((l) => (
                  <a class="btn btn-outline btn-primary" href={l.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{l.label} →</a>
                ))}
              </div>
            )}
          </article>
        );
      })}
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>